  "hero.title": "How a CPU's Three Levels of Cache Work",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.sub": "Memory takes {dramNs} ns, and a CPU does several things every nanosecond.<br>How do caches of tens of kilobytes to tens of megabytes keep it from waiting most of the time?",
  "hero.lead": "The second question follows: why three levels, and why is each one the size it is? We start with one address: how it is cut into three parts and which set it lands in; when the set is full, what gets evicted; when written data is really sent on; how the three levels share the work; why L1 has stayed small for decades; how the prefetcher guesses the next line; how values stay current when several cores each hold a copy; and finally how to measure these steps on your own computer. <strong>Every model is a real cache, run one access at a time over a stream of addresses that the page generates</strong>, or a named textbook result (Mattson's reuse distance, Belady's optimal replacement, Hill's three kinds of miss, Chen and Baer's stride prefetching, the MESI protocol).",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. The capacities, way counts and latencies of real chips come from vendor documents (AMD's optimisation guide, and the slides AMD and Intel presented at Hot Chips 2024 and Intel's 2024 technology tour) and from public measurements (Chips and Cheese). Each one gives its source and date. <strong>Values marked “tuned parameter” were chosen so the models could compute them; they were not measured</strong>. The text says how far each conclusion moves when one of them is doubled. This page explains mechanisms only. <strong>It does not rate which chip is worth buying</strong>.",
  "ch1.title": "Why cache at all, and why more than one level",
  "toc.ch1": "Why cache at all, and why more than one level",
  "ch1.law": "Bigger storage is slower: longer wires and more places to compare. So we build several levels, small and fast nearest the core and large and slow further out, so that most accesses finish at the nearest level.",
  "ch1.p1": "A desktop processor runs at up to {ghz} GHz, so one cycle is under a fifth of a nanosecond, and each cycle can issue several instructions. Reading one value from memory takes about {dramNs} ns, or roughly {dram} cycles. If every read went to memory, the core would spend nearly all its time waiting. The full latency ladder from registers to memory is drawn in <a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch2'>Chapter 2 of “Why Compute Waits for Memory”</a>; this essay takes each rung apart.",
  "ch1.p2": "Why not build one large, fast cache? Because it cannot be done. The table below gives the latencies from vendor documents. AMD's Zen 5 L1 data cache is {zL1} KiB and returns data in {zL1lat} cycles; its L2 is {zL2} MiB at {zL2lat} cycles; its L3 is {zL3} MiB at an average of {zL3lat} cycles. Intel's Lion Cove, from 2024, grew L1 to {lcL1} KiB, and its latency rose to {lcL1lat} cycles, so Intel put a further level in front of it: an “L0” of {lcL0} KiB at {lcL0lat} cycles. The bigger the storage array, the longer the signals must travel and the more tags must be compared, and that does not fit in one cycle.",
  "ch1.th.chip": "Chip",
  "ch1.th.level": "Level",
  "ch1.th.size": "Size",
  "ch1.th.lat": "Latency",
  "ch1.p3": "The table shows a pattern: taking Zen 5, each cache level outwards is twenty to thirty-odd times larger and three or four times slower; going from L3 to memory is another eight or nine times slower. With levels stacked, the average access time is the share of accesses each level catches, multiplied by that level's latency, summed over the levels (the general formula for average access time is in <a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch4'>Chapter 4 of that essay</a>). Which level catches which accesses depends on the program. The program below alternates four kinds of work.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Switch each level on or off and see the average wait",
  "ch1.model.how": "The program alternates four kinds of access. {pStack} of accesses go to the stack and local variables, a {szStack} KiB region. {pWork} sweep again and again through a {szWork} KiB working array. {pTable} look up a table ({szTable} MiB by default; adjustable). {pStream} read a stream that never comes back. These shares and sizes are <strong>tuned parameters</strong>, chosen to stand for an ordinary program. Capacities and latencies are Zen 5's documented figures, and the {dramNs} ns of memory is converted to cycles at {ghz} GHz. Whether a level can hold a set of data follows from its LRU reuse distance (see below). The tests replay a real interleaved address sequence through the models, and the results agree.",
