  "ch1.say.none": "No cache: every access goes to memory, averaging <em>{amat}</em>.",
  "ch1.say.three": "All three levels on, lookup table {table}: only {mem} of accesses go to memory, averaging <em>{amat}</em>.",
  "ch1.say.threeSpill": "The lookup table, {table}, is already bigger than L3, so L3 cannot hold it either: {mem} of accesses go to memory, averaging <em>{amat}</em>. Shrink the table until it fits in L3, then look again.",
  "ch1.say.nol1": "No L1: even the small set of most-used data must come from L2 or further out, averaging <em>{amat}</em>, which is {x} times the figure with all three levels on ({best}).",
  "ch1.say.partial": "With this setup the average is <em>{amat}</em>, which is {x} times the figure with all three levels on ({best}); {mem} of accesses go to memory.",
  "ch1.p4": "With all three levels on, the average is {amat3} cycles per access. With only L1 it becomes {amat1}; only L3 gives {amatL3}; L1 with L2 gives {amat12}, and L1 with L3 gives {amat13}; with no cache at all it is {amat0}. Notably, with all three levels on, only {memShare} of accesses go to memory, yet they take {memPart} of the total time. One memory access costs as much as a hundred or more L1 hits, so cutting even a few memory accesses is worth another level.",
  "ch1.p5": "Which level catches which data depends on its <strong>reuse distance</strong>: the number of distinct lines touched between one use of a line and its next use (Mattson et al., 1970). In an LRU cache, a line survives exactly when that number is smaller than the number of lines the cache holds. In this program the stack's reuse distance is {dStack} lines and L1 holds {l1Lines} lines, so the stack lives in L1. The working array's distance is {dWork} lines: too big for L1, but it fits in L2. The lookup table's is {dTable} lines, which only L3 can hold. Push the table up to 32 MiB and it no longer fits even in L3; the average jumps at once to {amatBig} cycles, the same as having no L3. The benefit of a cache is not smooth: the boundary between fitting and not fitting is sharp.",
  "ch1.limit": "The model assumes each level is an ideal LRU and that each data set is swept in order, so every data set is either wholly in a level or wholly out of it. Real programs access data less regularly, and the steps are rounder. Latencies are the documented cycles from request to usable data; the model does not count the waiting that prefetching and out-of-order execution hide.",
  "ch2.title": "How an address is split",
  "toc.ch2": "How an address is split",
  "ch2.law": "A cache moves data in lines of 64 bytes. An address is cut into three parts: the lowest bits are the offset within the line, the middle bits pick the set, and the high bits form the tag, which identifies the line within its set.",
  "ch2.p1": "A cache never moves a single byte. Reading one byte brings in a whole 64-byte line. Zen 5, Intel and Neoverse V3 all use 64-byte lines; the Mac this page was written on reports {apLine} bytes. The neighbours come in free, which is why reading an array in order is fast. <a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch2'>Chapter 2 of that essay</a> covers the difference between walking rows and walking columns.",
  "ch2.p2": "A cache is not one big box. It is divided into many <strong>sets</strong>, each with several slots called <strong>ways</strong>. An address can go into only one fixed set. Take Zen 5's L1 data cache: {zL1} KiB in {zL1w} ways, with 64-byte lines, gives {z1sets} sets. So the lowest 6 bits pick the byte within the line, the next {z1idx} bits pick the set, and together these make the lowest {z1low} bits; everything above is tag. The L2 has {z2sets} sets, so its set index is {z2idx} bits. A CCX's L3 has {z3sets} sets, with a {z3idx}-bit index, and the lowest {z3low} bits are all used to locate a byte.",
  "dg.split.cap": "How Zen 5's L1 data cache splits an address: {zL1} KiB, {zL1w}-way, 64-byte lines, giving {z1sets} sets. Bit positions are for a 48-bit virtual address; the tag actually compared comes from the translated physical address (Chapter 7).",
  "dg.split.aria": "Diagram: Zen 5 L1 data cache splits a 48-bit address into tag, set index and offset",
