  "ch2.model.title": "Split an address",
  "ch2.model.how": "Type any hexadecimal address, pick a cache level, and see it cut into tag, set index and offset. The real L3 scrambles the address before spreading it across its slices (Chapter 6); here the simple textbook split is used.",
  "ch2.ctl.addr": "Addr",
  "ch2.ctl.cache": "Cache",
  "ch2.cfg.l1": "Zen 5 L1D",
  "ch2.cfg.l2": "Zen 5 L2",
  "ch2.cfg.l3": "Zen 5 L3",
  "ch2.cfg.toy": "Toy cache",
  "ch2.read.tag": "Tag",
  "ch2.read.index": "Set index",
  "ch2.read.offset": "Offset within line (bytes)",
  "ch2.read.sets": "Sets at this level",
  "ch2.p3": "The set index is taken straight from the address, so two addresses that differ by a whole multiple of (number of sets × line size) land in the same set. A tiny cache makes this clear: 128 bytes, 16-byte lines, direct-mapped (one slot per set), 8 sets in all. The program computes the dot product of two arrays.",
  "ch2.model2.title": "A dot product on a toy cache",
  "ch2.model2.how": "a and b each hold 8 4-byte numbers, and a[i] and b[i] are read alternately. Choose where b starts and the number of ways, then step through to see which set each access falls in, and whether it hits or misses.",
  "ch2.code.aria": "Code of the dot-product loop",
  "ch2.code": "float x = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)\n    x += a[i] * b[i];   // a starts at 0x100; b starts at 0x180 or 0x1A0",
  "ch2.ctl.b": "Start of b",
  "ch2.ctl.ways": "Ways",
  "ch2.ways.1": "Direct-mapped",
